Use the based signed Gauss code $-1,+3,-2,+1,-3,+2$, where a negative visit is
under, a positive visit is over, and the separate algebraic crossing-sign
vector is $(+1,+1,+1)$. This is a cyclic shift and the relabeling $2\leftrightarrow
3$ of the right-trefoil signed Gauss code. Its normalized PD records in
clockwise order beginning with outgoing over-dart are
$$X_1=(e_1,e_2,e_3,e_4),\qquad X_2=(e_2,e_5,e_6,e_3),\qquad X_3=(e_5,e_1,e_4,e_6).$$
Its face cycles are
$$F_0=(1:e_1,2:e_2,3:e_5),\quad F_1=(1:e_2,2:e_3),\quad F_2=(1:e_3,3:e_4,2:e_6),\quad F_3=(1:e_4,3:e_1),\quad F_4=(2:e_5,3:e_6).$$
Selecting $F_0$ as the outer face gives a valid one-component three-crossing
code, and the bounded enumerator includes its reconstructed candidate at
crossing bound $3$.

The figure displays the positive two-strand braid closure used for this code.
The braid strands are oriented downward, the closure arcs upward, and the
crossings are labeled from top to bottom $1,3,2$; the upper branch is the
right-to-left branch at each positive crossing. The proof below verifies the
code and face cycles from finite dart data and does not infer them from the
drawing.

```tikz
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.8,>=stealth]
  % Top crossing, label 1: under branch left-to-right, over branch right-to-left.
  \draw[white,line width=5pt] (-1,0) -- (1,-1);
  \draw[->] (-1,0) -- (-0.18,-0.41);
  \draw[->] (0.18,-0.59) -- (1,-1);
  \draw[->,line width=1.2pt] (1,0) -- (-1,-1);
  \node at (0.34,-0.5) {$+1$};
  % Middle crossing, label 3.
  \draw[white,line width=5pt] (-1,-1) -- (1,-2);
  \draw[->] (-1,-1) -- (-0.18,-1.41);
  \draw[->] (0.18,-1.59) -- (1,-2);
  \draw[->,line width=1.2pt] (1,-1) -- (-1,-2);
  \node at (0.34,-1.5) {$+3$};
  % Bottom crossing, label 2.
  \draw[white,line width=5pt] (-1,-2) -- (1,-3);
  \draw[->] (-1,-2) -- (-0.18,-2.41);
  \draw[->] (0.18,-2.59) -- (1,-3);
  \draw[->,line width=1.2pt] (1,-2) -- (-1,-3);
  \node at (0.34,-2.5) {$+2$};
  % The two closure arcs join matching left and right endpoints outside the braid.
  \draw[->] (1,-3) .. controls (2.1,-3) and (2.1,0) .. (1,0);
  \draw[->] (-1,-3) .. controls (-2.1,-3) and (-2.1,0) .. (-1,0);
\end{tikzpicture}
```

A **quandle** is a set $Q$ with a binary operation $*:Q\times Q\to Q$ such that:

1. $x*x=x$ for every $x\in Q$;
2. for every $y\in Q$, the right translation $R_y:Q\to Q$, $R_y(x)=x*y$, is a bijection;
3. $(x*y)*z=(x*z)*(y*z)$ for all $x,y,z\in Q$.

Write $x\mathbin{\bar *}y:=R_y^{-1}(x)$ for the unique element satisfying
$(x\mathbin{\bar *}y)*y=x$. Let $D$ be a regular oriented link diagram. An
**arc** of $D$ is a connected strand segment cut at each crossing where that
strand passes under; an overstrand continues through its crossing. Orient the
projection plane. Along an oriented overarc with tangent $t$, let $n$ be the
unit normal in the plane for which $(t,n)$ is positively oriented. At each
crossing, call the underarc from which $n$ points the source underarc and the
underarc toward which $n$ points the target underarc. A **quandle coloring**
of $D$ by $Q$ is an assignment $c$ of an element of $Q$ to every arc such
that, at each crossing, if the source, over, and target colors are $a,b,d$,
then $d=a*b$. Equivalently, when the source and target are named in the
opposite order, $a=d\mathbin{\bar *}b$. Write $\operatorname{Col}_Q(D)$ for
the set of all such assignments.

The local convention is shown below. The overarc is oriented from southwest
to northeast, so its positive normal points northwest; the underarc is
oriented from southeast to northwest. Thus $x$ is the source color, $y$ is
the overarc color, and $z=x*y$ is the target color. The gap marks the
underpassing strand.

```tikz
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1]
  \draw[->] (1.15,-1.15) -- (0.18,-0.18);
  \draw[->] (-0.18,0.18) -- (-1.15,1.15);
  \draw[->,line width=1.2pt] (-1.15,-1.15) -- (1.15,1.15);
  \node at (0.92,-0.82) {$x$};
  \node at (-0.82,-0.82) {$y$};
  \node at (-0.82,0.92) {$z$};
  \node at (0.35,0.35) {$z=x*y$};
\end{tikzpicture}
```

Let $A$ be an indeterminate and let $\mathbb Z[A^{\pm1}]$ be the Laurent
polynomial ring in $A$. A **nonempty unoriented regular link diagram** is the
finite planar projection and over/under data of a regular oriented link diagram
after its strand arrows are forgotten. Its finite crossing set is denoted
$C(D)$, and at least one closed component is required. The empty link diagram is
outside this definition.

At a crossing use the following local chart: the over-strand runs from the
southwest endpoint to the northeast endpoint and is drawn continuously over the
under-strand, which runs from northwest to southeast with a gap at the
intersection. Horizontal-right followed by vertical-up is the positive
orientation of this displayed chart. In this chart the **A-smoothing** joins the
two left endpoints and joins the two right endpoints; the **B-smoothing** joins
the two top endpoints and joins the two bottom endpoints. These labels are
attached to this displayed chart and are transported by orientation-preserving
changes of local coordinates. Thus turning right from an entering underpassing
half-strand gives the A-smoothing.

```tikz
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.72, line cap=round, line join=round]
  \begin{scope}
    \draw[thick] (-1,1)--(-0.14,0.14);
    \draw[thick] (0.14,-0.14)--(1,-1);
    \draw[very thick] (-1,-1)--(1,1);
    \node at (0,-1.45) {crossing};
  \end{scope}
  \begin{scope}[xshift=3.4cm]
    \draw[thick] (-1,1) .. controls (-0.45,0.35) and (-0.45,-0.35) .. (-1,-1);
    \draw[thick] (1,1) .. controls (0.45,0.35) and (0.45,-0.35) .. (1,-1);
    \node at (0,-1.45) {$A$-smoothing, weight $A$};
  \end{scope}
  \begin{scope}[xshift=6.8cm]
    \draw[thick] (-1,1) .. controls (-0.35,0.45) and (0.35,0.45) .. (1,1);
    \draw[thick] (-1,-1) .. controls (-0.35,-0.45) and (0.35,-0.45) .. (1,-1);
    \node at (0,-1.45) {$B$-smoothing, weight $A^{-1}$};
  \end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
```

For a state $S:C(D)\to\{A,B\}$, let $a(S)$ and $b(S)$ be the numbers of
A- and B-smoothings, and let $|S|$ be the number of closed circles in the
crossingless diagram obtained by performing all smoothings. Set
$$\delta:=-A^2-A^{-2}$$
and define the **Kauffman bracket** of $D$ by
$$\langle D\rangle:=\sum_{S:C(D)\to\{A,B\}} A^{a(S)-b(S)}\delta^{|S|-1}\in\mathbb Z[A^{\pm1}].$$
Because $D$ is nonempty, every complete smoothing has $|S|\ge1$, so the loop
exponent is nonnegative. A crossingless $k$-component unlink with $k\ge1$ has
bracket $\delta^{k-1}$.

Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice for the final knot-type distinction. A diagram with $c=8$ crossings has $2^8=256$ Kauffman bracket smoothing states. For the trefoil diagram below, label its three Fox arcs $x_1,x_2,x_3$ so the crossing triples are: at $C_1$, over $x_1$ and under $x_2,x_3$; at $C_2$, over $x_2$ and under $x_3,x_1$; at $C_3$, over $x_3$ and under $x_1,x_2$. Thus the crossing equations are $2x_1-x_2-x_3=0$, $2x_2-x_3-x_1=0$, and $2x_3-x_1-x_2=0$. Over $\mathbb F_3$ the system has nine colorings. The crossingless unknot has one arc and three Fox 3-colorings; because the crossing equation is independent of strand orientations, this count difference distinguishes the underlying knot types.

```tikz
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.82,line cap=round,line join=round,>=stealth]
  % The outside arcs close the three-crossing two-strand braid.
  \draw[->,thick] (-1,-3) .. controls (-2.45,-3) and (-2.45,3) .. (-1,3);
  \draw[->,thick] (1,-3) .. controls (2.45,-3) and (2.45,3) .. (1,3);

  % C1: the upper-left to lower-right strand is over.
  \draw[->,thick] (1,3) -- (0.13,2.13);
  \draw[->,thick] (-0.13,1.87) -- (-1,1);
  \draw[->,very thick] (-1,3) -- (1,1);

  % C2: the upper-left to lower-right strand is over.
  \draw[->,thick] (1,1) -- (0.13,0.13);
  \draw[->,thick] (-0.13,-0.13) -- (-1,-1);
  \draw[->,very thick] (-1,1) -- (1,-1);

  % C3: the upper-left to lower-right strand is over.
  \draw[->,thick] (1,-1) -- (0.13,-1.87);
  \draw[->,thick] (-0.13,-2.13) -- (-1,-3);
  \draw[->,very thick] (-1,-1) -- (1,-3);

  \node[above left] at (0,2) {$C_1$};
  \node[above left] at (0,0) {$C_2$};
  \node[above left] at (0,-2) {$C_3$};
  \node at (-0.54,2.52) {$x_1$};
  \node at (-0.54,0.52) {$x_2$};
  \node at (-0.54,-1.48) {$x_3$};
\end{tikzpicture}
```

The figure is the closure of a two-strand braid with three identical crossings. Each braid strand is oriented downward, and each outside closure arc is oriented upward. At each crossing the upper-left to lower-right strand is continuous and over; the other strand has a gap at the crossing. The labels $x_1,x_2,x_3$ mark the three arcs between undercrossings, and the crossing incidences used in the statement specify the exact matrix independently of the drawing.

For every valid connected one-component oriented planar knot code with $n$ crossings together with a specified polygonal realization $P$ whose regular projection has that code, there is an explicit finite combinatorial construction of a triangulated $S^3$ containing that same $P$ as a subcomplex. An abstract valid code can first be realized by the stated reconstruction procedure and then used as $P$. A **good exterior triangulation** is the triangulation inherited on the complement of the interior of a regular neighborhood made from one fixed ball around each vertex of $P$ and one fixed tube block along each edge, with its boundary torus triangulated and a meridian path marked. The construction gives a good exterior triangulation with at most $C(n+1)$ tetrahedra for one universal constant $C$, and the marked meridian is a path of at most $C(n+1)$ boundary edges. At $n=0$ the construction uses a fixed solid-torus exterior triangulation.

A local crossing is lifted by separating the over- and under-strands in the interval direction; the schematic below records only the projected crossing, with the continuous strand passing over the gapped strand.

~~~tikz
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.15]
\draw[very thick] (-1,-1)--(1,1);
\draw[white,line width=7pt] (-1,1)--(1,-1);
\draw[thick] (-1,1)--(-0.18,0.18);
\draw[thick] (0.18,-0.18)--(1,-1);
\node at (0.62,0.78) {over};
\node at (-0.70,0.76) {under};
\end{tikzpicture}
~~~

The picture records only the planar local incidence. The proof constructs disjoint arcs in a three-dimensional crossing block and does not infer embeddedness from the drawing.

Let $\sigma_i$ be the positive geometric Artin braid crossing in a braid rectangle whose parameter increases from bottom to top, with strand $i$ passing below and strand $i+1$ passing above. Use the bracket chart in which the over-strand runs southwest to northeast, the $A$-smoothing joins left to left and right to right with weight $A$, and the $B$-smoothing joins top to top and bottom to bottom with weight $A^{-1}$. Let $I$ be the through pairing and let $e_i$ be the pairing with a top cap and a bottom cup at positions $i,i+1$. Then the positive crossing has bracket operator $A I+A^{-1}e_i$, and the negative crossing has bracket operator $A^{-1}I+A e_i$.

```tikz
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.82, line cap=round, line join=round]
  \begin{scope}
    \draw[thick] (-0.55,-1)--(-0.55,1);
    \draw[thick] (0.55,-1)--(0.55,1);
    \node at (0,1.35) {$I$};
  \end{scope}
  \begin{scope}[xshift=2.6cm]
    \draw[thick] (-0.55,1) .. controls (0.25,0.55) and (0.25,-0.55) .. (0.55,-1);
    \draw[thick] (0.55,1) .. controls (-0.25,0.55) and (-0.25,-0.55) .. (-0.55,-1);
    \node at (0,1.35) {$e_i$};
  \end{scope}
  \node at (1.25,0) {$\sigma_i=A I+A^{-1}e_i$};
\end{tikzpicture}
```

Assume full AC. The positive one-crossing type-I kink of the unknot is connected and alternating but not reduced; its diagram has one crossing while the represented unknot has crossing number zero, and its normalized library Jones polynomial has $t$-span zero.

```tikz
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9, line cap=round, line join=round]
  \draw[->,thick] (-0.22,-0.22)--(-0.045,-0.045);
  \draw[->,thick] (0.045,0.045)--(0.22,0.22);
  \draw[->,very thick] (-0.22,0.22)--(0.22,-0.22);
  \draw[->,thick] (0.22,0.22) .. controls (0.95,1.0) and (-0.95,1.0) .. (-0.22,0.22);
  \draw[->,thick] (0.22,-0.22) .. controls (0.95,-1.0) and (-0.95,-1.0) .. (-0.22,-0.22);
  \node at (0,1.25) {positive type-I kink};
\end{tikzpicture}
```

The arrows orient the single component; at the central crossing, the diagonal from northwest to southeast is over and the southwest-to-northeast diagonal has a gap.

Assume full AC. The positive trefoil is represented by the closure of the three-crossing two-strand braid $\sigma_1^3$; its library Jones polynomial is $t+t^3-t^4$, of span $4-1=3$, and its displayed connected reduced alternating diagram has crossing number $3$. The figure-eight knot is represented by the closure of the four-crossing three-strand braid whose levels from bottom to top are $\sigma_2^{-1},\sigma_1,\sigma_2^{-1},\sigma_1$; its library Jones polynomial is $t^{-2}-t^{-1}+1-t+t^2$, of span $2-(-2)=4$, and its displayed connected reduced alternating diagram has crossing number $4$. The convention is $t=A^{-4}$.

```tikz
